#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.14"
# dependencies = [
#   "click>=8.1.0",
# ]
# ///

"""Report whether the two oracles agree (R6; plan step 10; task 28-oracle-parity).

``just oracle-parity`` is the single pipeline task.md describes as one piece of work: for every
row of ``docs/ORACLE.md``'s configuration table, and every fixture that row names, it slices once
with the pinned release binary (``oracle/slice``) and once with the locally built library
(``oracle/stage-dump``'s ``stage_dump``, unarmed -- the hook is never registered), compares the
three artifacts the release side can export (G-code, the ``--export-settings`` JSON, the
``--export-statistics`` JSON) with ``crates/oracle-compare`` through ``just compare ... --format
json`` -- the stable-keyed JSON ``format.rs``'s own doc comment says exists *for* the scripts of
steps 6, 10 and 25-27, never the prose ``--format text`` would otherwise have to be re-parsed from
-- and regenerates ``port/reports/oracle-parity.tsv`` (the file tasks 18 and 41-43 read at run
time) and ``port/reports/oracle-parity.md`` (the human-readable report, carrying a provenance
header and this run's own built-oracle CMake flags) from the measurement.

**The built oracle's run shape is always unarmed.** Every ``stage_dump`` invocation below passes
``--unarmed``; there is no code path in this file that registers the pipeline hook (``grep -n
'armed' oracle/parity`` shows only this flag and the comments explaining it).

**Which driver each side runs, per row.** ``port/goldens/configs/<configuration>.json``'s
existence is this script's own signal for whether ``docs/ORACLE.md`` marks that row "not
constructible through the pinned CLI" (task ``12-config-goldens`` only ever produces a golden for
a row the pinned CLI *could* construct, by running that CLI -- there is no other way a golden
comes to exist). When the golden exists: the release side runs
``oracle/slice <fixture> <configuration> --export-settings --export-statistics``, and the built
side runs ``stage_dump <fixture> <configuration> --unarmed --config <golden>``. When it does not:
no release-side run is attempted at all (there is no golden to reproduce and no other way to drive
the pinned CLI at this configuration), and the built side runs ``stage_dump <fixture>
<configuration> --unarmed --set <key>=<value> ...`` from the row's own ``Overrides`` cell (C12's
accepted fallback). Every row ``docs/ORACLE.md`` names today has a golden, so the second branch is
implemented but **not exercised by this run** -- said here and in the pull request, not claimed as
measured.

**What "level reached" means, and the L0-or-L1 reading of "reaches L1".** For each artifact this
script asks ``just compare`` to evaluate every level independently (R4: the levels are not assumed
nested) and reports the *strictest* (lowest-index) level whose outcome is a pass, scanning L0
before L1 before L2 and so on; ``none`` when no level passed at all. A pair that is byte-identical
(L0) has, in the vocabulary this report also uses for "reaches L1", reached it: L0 implies L1.
``normative oracle`` is ``release`` for a configuration-and-fixture pair only when *every one* of
its three artifact rows reached L0 or L1, and ``built`` otherwise -- stated here because an ``L0``
cell beside a ``built`` normative-oracle cell on some *other* pair's row would otherwise read as a
contradiction rather than as "a different pair failed to reach it".

**Idempotence and a real run's own non-determinism.** Re-running this script must leave both
committed files unchanged apart from their generated-at timestamp (the task's own "Done when").
Two hazards, both measured empirically against this branch's own first real runs, not assumed:

1. **L0's own failing location is never committed.** L0 compares raw bytes, before R4's own
   declared G-code normalisation strips the oracle's ``; generated by ... at HH:MM:SS`` line --
   and the release and the built oracle are two separate process invocations whose own copies of
   that line only sometimes land in the same wall-clock second. Three consecutive runs of this
   script, against rows ``port/reports/determinism.tsv`` itself gates ``L1`` (release-vs-release
   deterministic), produced three different L0 byte offsets for the same row: the number
   reflects how two processes happened to race, not a property of the row, so it is never
   committed literally (see ``gcode_differences``). L1 onward evaluate the already-normalised
   streams and carry none of this hazard.
2. **A row's own content can be non-deterministic beyond the clock**, measured by this script's
   sibling task on the release side alone (``fuzzy-skin``'s unseeded perimeter seam,
   ``port/reports/determinism.tsv``'s ``gate`` column marking it ``none``) -- the *built* oracle's
   own run-to-run determinism is never measured anywhere in this repository, so this withholding
   rule is keyed on the release oracle's gate alone; a later task may need a built-side gate too.
   For a (configuration, artifact) pair marked ``none`` there, this script commits only the
   ``none`` category it measures for level-reached -- itself still a category, not the specific
   literal failing value -- and sends this run's own literal detail to stdout instead, never into
   the committed report (stdout is not a committed file, so printing the real location there
   costs nothing against idempotence).

**What this script never touches.** ``port/reports/divergences.md`` (that file is written by the
audit regeneration, which reads this script's own ``.tsv`` as one of its four named sources, not by
hand here); ``port/goldens/`` (this script only *reads* a configuration's already-committed
golden); ``docs/ORACLE.md`` (unlike ``oracle/determinism``, this script adds no gate table there --
task.md names only the two ``port/reports/oracle-parity.*`` files as its own output); and
``vendor/OrcaSlicer/`` (nothing here ever writes into the vendored tree; the vendored libslic3r
this script links against is a precondition, built once by ``just oracle-build``, never rebuilt by
this script -- see ``build_stage_dump`` below, which fails loudly naming that recipe when the
archives it needs are missing, rather than rebuilding from empty or falling back to the release
binary alone).
"""

